cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code, dataset, or paper, please cite it as below."
title: "Plan-Then-Compile: Turning a General-Purpose Coder Model into a FHIR Data Analyst"
type: software
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-08-30"
license:
  - Apache-2.0
  - CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/adelelsayed/fhirsql-reasoning-sql"
abstract: >-
  A frozen Qwen2.5-Coder-14B-Instruct is effectively unusable as an analyst over
  a FHIR-derived clinical database: asked ordinary hospital questions it returns
  the right answer roughly one time in ten on familiar clinical concepts, never
  reproduces a correct query verbatim, and refuses unanswerable questions barely
  better than chance. This study takes that model to a working analyst.
  Fine-tuning with DoRA on 10,696 examples (9,680 with execution-verified gold
  SQL plus 1,016 unanswerable questions) raises execution correctness on a
  disjoint held-out patient population to 100.0%, and on clinical concepts
  appearing nowhere in training to 89.1%, reproducibly across three seeds;
  abstention on trained refusal categories reaches 100%/100%. Against a frozen
  baseline given the same schema information the fine-tuned models had
  internalized, those are rises from 34.8% and 60.8%; a dedicated ablation
  isolates the effect of that schema description and finds that merely making
  the terminology table visible drives the untrained model's code-hardcoding to
  zero. The study is explicit about evaluation-arm limits: the familiar arm is
  96.5% verbatim training rows and the unanswerable set is reused across splits,
  so those results show retention, while the unseen-concept arm shares no
  question, query or concept with training and is where transfer is
  established; its 89.1% is an upper bound, with text-match metrics giving 81.4%
  as a lower bound. The broader implication is a repeatable recipe for
  converting mid-sized open-weight models into competent domain analysts for
  specialized, terminology-heavy systems that general-purpose models handle
  poorly.
